        "description": "<div id=\"remove_no_follow\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column\">\n\t\t\t\t\t  <div class=\"col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"article-column__content\">\n<section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>There have been plenty of warnings about job losses due to AI, <a href=\"https://www.cio.com/article/4012162/ai-begins-to-reshape-the-it-job-landscape-as-layoffs-rise.html\">particularly in the world of IT</a> and in the <a href=\"https://www.computerworld.com/article/3970031/two-thirds-of-jobs-will-be-impacted-by-ai.html\">reduction of entry-level positions</a>. Doom mongers\u2019 claims that AI is going to eradicate all our jobs look to be exaggerated but there is little room for complacency as there are some roles most definitely at risk \u2014 and software engineers, in particular, should be looking over their shoulders.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Economists Luis Garicano, Jin Li, and Yanhui Wu, report in <a href=\"https:/",
